    "n": "04",
    "title": "Who should be baptized, and how?",
    "blocks": [
     {
      "k": "read",
      "runs": [
       "The site holds with article VII that “Christian baptism is the immersion of a believer in water in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.” There are three reasons. The command in ",
       {
        "r": "MAT.28.19",
        "t": "Matthew 28:19"
       },
       " puts making disciples first. Acts shows the pattern traced in part 03. And ",
       {
        "r": "ROM.6",
        "t": "Romans 6"
       },
       " and ",
       {
        "r": "COL.2",
        "t": "Colossians 2"
       },
       " describe baptism as a burial and a rising. Older Baptists said the same. The Second London Baptist Confession (1677) says that those who “do actually profess repentance towards God, faith in, and obedience to, our Lord Jesus, are the only proper subjects of this ordinance”, and that immersion “is necessary to the due administration of this ordinance” (chapter 29, sections 2 and 4). The New Hampshire Confession (1833) calls baptism “the immersion in water of a believer”."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "other",
      "runs": [
       "Presbyterian and Reformed churches teach otherwise, from a covenant argument. The Westminster Confession says that “Not only those that do actually profess faith in and obedience unto Christ, but also the infants of one or both believing parents, are to be baptized” (chapter 28, section 4). It also says that “Dipping of the person into the water is not necessary; but Baptism is rightly administered by pouring or sprinkling water upon the person” (28.3). They reason this way. God gave circumcision to Abraham’s children as infants. ",
       {
        "r": "COL.2.11-12",
        "t": "Colossians 2:11–12"
       },
       " sets circumcision and baptism side by side. So the children of believers should receive the covenant sign now, as they did then. ",
       {
        "r": "COL.2.11-12",
        "t": "Colossians 2:11–12"
       },
       " is among the Bible verses cited for 28.4. Some commentators from these churches also read the households of Acts this way. Albert Barnes, a Presbyterian, finds in Lydia’s household “a strong presumptive proof that this was an instance of household or infant baptism” (on ",
       {
        "r": "ACT.16.15",
        "t": "Acts 16:15"
       },
       "). Jamieson, Fausset and Brown, also not Baptists, say on the same verse that whether children were present “is not explicitly stated”, and that “the question of infant baptism must be determined on other grounds”."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "read",
      "runs": [
       "The Baptist answer comes from the same verses. The circumcision Colossians speaks of is “performed by Christ and not by human hands” (2:11). What baptism answers to is that inward change, and the rising is “through your faith” (2:12). Circumcision marked birth into a family line. The new covenant people are marked by new birth and faith, and that is what baptism is said to show. As for the households, Acts says the jailer’s household “had come to believe” and that Crispus “and his whole household believed” (",
       {
        "r": "ACT.16.34",
        "t": "Acts 16:34"
       },
       "; ",
       {
        "r": "ACT.18.8",
        "t": "18:8"
       },
       "). Lydia’s verse says nothing either way. These verses cannot settle the question for either side on their own, so Baptists rest it on the command and the pattern."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "other",
      "runs": [
       "The Orthodox churches baptize infants, and they baptize by immersing the child three times, once at each name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. So Baptists and Orthodox agree that baptism means going under the water. They differ on who is to be baptized, not how."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "h3",
      "t": "“Do I need to be baptized again?”"
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "Many people come to a Baptist church having been baptized as babies, often by parents who loved them and loved Christ. A Baptist church will ask such a person to be baptized as a believer. It does this because it holds that infant baptism is not the baptism Scripture describes. It never does so because it doubts the faith of the parents who brought the child. Most Baptists would not call this “re-baptism” at all. In their eyes, it is the first time the person receives the baptism Christ commanded. If your parents brought you to be baptized, you can honour their love and their prayers, and still take the step yourself."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "h3",
      "t": "“When is a child ready?”"
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "The confession sets no age. Baptist churches usually talk with a child who asks, to hear in the child’s own words what they believe about Jesus. Read more in ",
       {
        "s": null,
        "t": "Children and the home"
       },
       "."
      ]
     }
    ],
    "readingsText": [
     {
      "r": "COL.2.11-12",
      "t": "Colossians 2:11–12"
     },
     " and the households of ",
     {
      "r": "ACT.16",
      "t": "Acts 16"
     },
     " (from parts 02 and 03)"
    ],
    "readings": [
     "COL.2.11-12",
     "ACT.16"
    ],
    "hook": [
     "Christians who agree on almost everything else still part at the water’s edge."
    ],
    "consider": [
     "In ",
     {
      "r": "COL.2.11-12",
      "t": "Colossians 2:11–12"
     },
     ", what kind of circumcision does baptism answer to? Why can neither side settle the question from the household verses alone?"
    ],
